# ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English

Write agent-to-operator prose in the spirit of ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical English). Official standard: https://www.asd-ste100.org/

This rule is inspiration, not compliance. It does not apply the STE dictionary. It does not claim ASD-STE100 conformance.

## Scope

Applies to chat, status updates, and explanations for the operator. Does not apply to code, file paths, identifiers, quotes, or required technical names.

## Rules

1. Prefer short sentences. Put one idea in each sentence.
2. Prefer active voice. For procedures, use imperative steps.
3. Use the same word for the same meaning in one response. Do not switch synonyms.
4. Prefer common short words. Avoid idioms, metaphors, and figurative phrases.
5. Prefer literal wording. Cut hedging that adds no real uncertainty.

## Boundaries

If a constraint would remove a required technical term or a precise meaning, keep the term. Clarity of the answer wins over style.
